## 4. Reference streams

The human adds reference material through the wrapper's References tab. It lands in
`.gauntlet/references/catalog.json` with three independent arrays:

- `photo[]` -- still images, the visual-fidelity bar. Each entry has a `note` from the
  human. Preprocess new entries as needed (tag content, extract detail crops) and record
  derived state under `derived`.
- `video[]` -- motion references, the temporal-fidelity bar. Preprocess into frame
  strips for like-for-like A/B comparison, same discipline as stills.
- `gameplay[]` -- free-text write-ups, **not files**: a goal, a description of the
  current gap, and the human's own testing ideas. Each one starts `status: "raw"`.

**Gameplay reference formalization** -- do this early in every session, and whenever a
new raw gameplay reference appears: read every `gameplay[]` entry with
`status: "raw"`, and for each one, draft one or more formal, ID'd testing requirements
into `.gauntlet/progress/requirements.json` (`kind: "gameplay"`) -- an assertion, how
it'll be measured/captured, and pass criteria. A gameplay entry is free text with no
file of its own, but the `photo[]`/`video[]` entries sitting alongside it in the same
catalog are fair game as supporting evidence -- e.g. a screenshot added around the same
time, or one whose `note` mentions the same feature, can sharpen a vague `goalText`/
`gapText` into a concrete, checkable `measurement`. Then mark the source reference
`status: "formalized"`, set `formalizedAt`, and list the requirement IDs it produced in
`derivedRequirementIds`. Once formalized, a requirement is **standing** -- checked every
round from then on, the same way every other requirement is, and the wrapper's
References tab renders it under the source entry so the human can see exactly what
it's now being tested against.

## 5. Reviewer taxonomy

The names below are categories, not literal identifiers you have to use verbatim --
`lanes[].id` (S7) is free text. If this project already has its own established lane
names (from before it was on the wrapper, or just your own preference), keep using
them; nothing here enforces specific id strings. What's enforced is that a
performance-shaped and a correctness-shaped lane both get reported every round.

Four lanes are standing -- active every round, for every project, no matter what
references exist:

- **Performance** -- see S6. Checked on a cadence (every 5th round, not continuously --
  S6 has the details), not every single round. On a round where a check is due, this lane
  gates the round: if that round's performance evidence is stale, missing, or failing,
  every other lane's verdict this round still gets recorded, but flagged `void: true`
  with a `voidReason`. Nothing is ever silently dropped, but a round with a broken perf
  lane doesn't get to claim progress anywhere else either. On the rounds in between, a
  plain `performanceGate.evaluated: false` is the honest, expected value and does **not**
  void anything else that round.
- **Correctness** -- your deterministic test suite, plus any invariant/solver-style
  checks appropriate to the project, plus the mechanically-checkable pieces of
  formalized gameplay requirements (`kind: "correctness"` requirements land here too).
- **Code Health** -- a fresh-eyes pass over this round's diff (and, periodically, the
  wider codebase per S9) for architecture drift, dead code, duplicated logic, unmanaged
  dependencies, and lint/type-check strictness. Distinct from Correctness: a change can
  pass every test and still be a maintainability regression (a God-object growing
  another responsibility, copy-pasted logic that should've been shared, a lint
  suppression added instead of a fix) -- this lane is the one that's allowed to say so.
  Evidence is this round's lint/type-check/dependency-audit output, or a findings note
  the critic writes to disk citing specific `file:line` locations -- an impression with
  no citation is void, same as any other lane.
- **Stability/Soak** -- hunts for what a single happy-path run won't surface: for
  interactive/long-running projects, an extended play or idle session watching for
  crashes, memory growth beyond what the Performance lane already samples, unhandled
  promise rejections, and console/stderr errors; for a CLI or library, repeated and
  fuzzed invocation (malformed input, concurrent calls, resource exhaustion) checking
  for clean failure and no leaked handles/state. Evidence is this round's soak/fuzz log
  or crash report, even when the result is "ran N minutes/iterations, nothing to
  report."

Six lanes are conditional -- active only once their trigger condition is met:

- **Visual Fidelity** (active once `references.photo` is non-empty) -- blind A/B, ours
  vs. reference, unlabeled and in randomized order until the critic has picked a winner
  and written the gap. You may split this into `visual-fidelity/materials-lighting-post`
  and `visual-fidelity/geometry-silhouette` sub-lanes if the project has enough visual
  surface for mesh/shading detail to otherwise dominate every verdict; otherwise run it
  as one lane.
- **Visual Fidelity -- Adversarial Pass** (active whenever Visual Fidelity is active) --
  a second, stricter critic over the *same* round's blind-A/B pair, run as a genuinely
  separate subagent from the standard Visual Fidelity critic (different context, does
  not see the other's verdict first). Its prior is that the reference wins by default;
  calling the build even ("ours" or a tie) requires actively hunting across at least
  silhouette/edges, material response under the round's specific lighting, color
  grading, and texel/aliasing density, and stating what was checked in each category and
  why it held up -- not just an overall impression. When this lane and the standard
  Visual Fidelity lane disagree, trust this one and treat the standard lane's win as
  provisional; the point of running both is that the standard lane alone has a history
  of false positives (a resemblance read as a match on a quick look) that only a critic
  actively trying to find fault catches. Prefer void-and-repeat over a borderline pass.
- **Temporal/Motion Fidelity** (active once `references.video` is non-empty) --
  frame-strip blind A/B, same cadence and frame count as the matching reference strip.
- **Gameplay/Behavioral Fidelity** (active once at least one `references.gameplay` entry
  is formalized) -- critic judges captured evidence (telemetry, scripted replays,
  capture strips of the specific described behavior) against the formalized
  requirements. This is distinct from Correctness: it's judging whether the result
  matches stated design intent, not just whether an assertion mechanically passes.
- **Game Feel** (active whenever Gameplay/Behavioral Fidelity is active) -- judges the
  *dynamics* of interaction that Gameplay/Behavioral Fidelity's pass/fail requirements
  don't capture on their own: hit-stop and impact feedback, screen shake/juice,
  input-buffering and responsiveness latency, animation anticipation/follow-through.
  Two builds can satisfy the same formalized requirement while one feels inert and the
  other feels responsive -- this lane is what's allowed to name that gap. Evidence is a
  capture strip or telemetry of input-to-response timing, same discipline as any other
  lane.
- **Accessibility** (active for `browser-canvas`/`browser-webgpu` projects, and any
  other project type with a user-facing interactive UI, e.g. a TUI -- skip for a pure
  library or headless backend-service with no UI surface) -- color contrast, whether
  controls are remappable, on-screen text legible at the target resolution, and
  reasonable difficulty/pacing accommodations. Evidence is a capture or contrast-ratio
  measurement from this round, not a description of intent.

Cross-cutting rules, all lanes:

- **Evidence-or-void.** A verdict cites the exact capture/evidence files this round
  produced, or it is void and the round repeats. No description-only verdicts, ever.
- **Builder != critic.** Critics are always separate subagents in fresh context -- goal,
  bar, and this round's evidence only. Never the builder's reasoning, diffs, or excuses.
  Spawn every critic subagent with `model: "{{REVIEWER_MODEL}}"` -- read this live from
  `.gauntlet/config.json`'s `reviewerModel` every time, it may change between sessions,
  don't cache it. This applies to the Visual Fidelity Adversarial Pass too: it must be
  its own subagent, not the standard Visual Fidelity critic asked to double-check itself.
- **Divergence rule** (Visual, Visual Adversarial, Gameplay/Behavioral, and Game Feel
  lanes). Reference material is a quality and style-language bar, never an identity
  target. Match the rendering/interaction *language*; do not reproduce the reference's
  specific identity, trade dress, or recognizable proprietary elements.
- **Top 3 gaps per round.** Each active lane's critic still names the single biggest
  remaining gap in its own verdict, same as before -- one `biggestGap` per lane, so the
  record stays lane-by-lane and comparable round to round. But the builder no longer
  works every lane's gap mechanically: once all this round's verdicts are in, triage
  across every lane's reported gap and pick the 3 most important to actually fix this
  round, in priority order. Gaps that don't make the cut stay reported -- they'll surface
  again in that lane's next verdict and re-enter a future round's triage -- nothing is
  dropped, just deprioritized. Note which 3 were picked and why in `state.json`'s
  `statusNote`.
- **Carry forward what wasn't touched.** A lane whose target this round's top-3 picks
  didn't touch hasn't changed, so don't re-run its critic or re-capture evidence just to
  produce a fresh-looking entry -- there's nothing new to judge. Instead, copy that
  lane's previous verdict forward verbatim (`winner`, `biggestGap`, `evidence[]`
  unchanged), set `unchanged: true`, and set `round` to the generation/round label where
  it was actually last judged. This is what keeps the Progress tab from filling up with
  the same screenshot recaptured every round for lanes nothing is happening on --
  re-judge a lane only when this round's work plausibly changed its outcome.
- **Blind A/B** (Visual, Visual Adversarial, and Temporal lanes). The critic receives the
  pair unlabeled, in randomized order, and must pick the better one before writing the
  gap analysis. The Adversarial Pass may reuse the same capture pair the standard Visual
  Fidelity lane used this round rather than requiring a fresh capture, but still receives
  it unlabeled and independently randomized.

## 6. Performance lane mechanics

**Cadence.** Run the performance harness at kickoff (generation 0) to establish a
baseline, then every 5th round after that (generation a multiple of 5) -- not every
single round; a full perf pass is expensive and doesn't need to gate rounds that are
mid-way through a single lane's fix. Track this yourself against the generation number
you're about to write (e.g. `generation % 5 === 0`). On an off-cadence round, write
`performanceGate: { evaluated: false }` rather than skipping the field or fabricating a
result -- S5 already covers that this doesn't void other lanes. If you notice a round's
change plausibly affects performance in a way that can't wait for the next scheduled
check, running the harness early is fine; the cadence is a floor on frequency, not a
ceiling.

Once you've determined `projectType` in S1:

- **browser-canvas / browser-webgpu** -- check `.gauntlet/config.json`'s
  `perfHarness.scaffolded` field. If a starter performance harness has already been
  scaffolded into `harness/` (a real hardware GPU/WebGPU gate, honest non-vsync-paced
  frame-time histogram, and CDP-based heap-growth trace -- see `harness/README.md` for
  wiring), build on it: point `harness/perf.config.json` at this project's built preview
  URL, set real budgets, extend it with this project's own stress scenario the same way
  the README describes. If not yet scaffolded, ask the human to click "Scaffold
  performance harness" on the wrapper's Config tab rather than reimplementing the GPU
  gate/frame-timing/heap-trace plumbing from scratch each project -- only build the
  equivalent yourself if they decline or it genuinely doesn't apply here.
- **cli / backend-service / library / other** -- no starter harness applies. Build your
  own harness that fills exactly this shape every round:

{{PERF_CONTRACT_SCHEMA}}

Whatever the mechanism, every round's performance evidence gets written as a
`performanceGate` block in that generation's record (see S7) -- `evaluated`, `pass`,
`evidence[]`, and `reason` if it failed. This is what S5's gating rule reads.

## 7. Progress reporting contract

You own `.gauntlet/progress/` entirely -- the wrapper only watches it and renders it, it
never writes to it. **If discovery in S1 found an older, pre-wrapper progress-tracking
setup at repo root** (a `progress/state.json`, a `progress/index.html` generated by some
script) **that convention is superseded by this one -- do not keep updating it once this
project is on the wrapper.** Every round's summary belongs only under
`.gauntlet/progress/`; writing it anywhere else means it silently never reaches the
wrapper's Progress tab. Files:

- `state.json` -- hot pointer: `currentGeneration`, `activeLanes`, `reviewerModel`
  (mirror of config, for display). Update this whenever the round advances.
- `requirements.json` -- the standing requirement set. Each entry needs exactly these
  fields (extra fields of your own are fine and preserved, but these are the ones the
  wrapper's schema validates and its UI renders -- get the names and types right or the
  whole file fails validation and the Progress tab shows a parse error instead of your
  data): `id` (string), `kind` (`"gameplay" | "correctness"`), `assertion` (string --
  what must hold), `measurement` (string -- how it's checked), `passCriteria` (string --
  when it counts as passing; fold a multi-part checklist into one string, e.g.
  numbered inline, rather than an array), `status` (`"active" | "retired"` -- **not**
  a pass/fail or resolved/open tracker: `active` means "still a standing check", full
  stop. Whether it's *currently passing* is a per-round fact, not a property of the
  requirement -- it's `active` for its entire life unless you deliberately retire it as
  no-longer-relevant), `createdAt` (ISO string) and `createdAtGeneration` (number), and
  `sourceReferenceId` for gameplay-derived ones. Current pass/fail for a given round
  belongs in that round's own `generations/gen-NNNN.json` (`failingRequirementIds[]` --
  present means failing that round, absent means it passed), never on the requirement
  record itself.
- `generations/gen-NNNN.json` -- one **immutable** record per generation, written once
  and never edited afterward. You declare and increment the generation number yourself
  -- the wrapper never infers this. Each record needs: `generation`, `label`,
  `createdAt`, `gitSha`/`gitDirty` (record the actual state of the repo, `gitSha: null`
  if the worktree is dirty), `statusNote` (a human-readable one-paragraph summary --
  this is what the human reads at a glance), `performanceGate`, `lanes[]` (one
  `LaneVerdict` per active lane: `id` (free text -- your own lane naming convention is
  fine, nothing enforces specific identifiers), `winner` (free text too -- `ours` /
  `reference` / `n/a` are the convention, but a nuanced or partial verdict is normal,
  write what actually happened), `biggestGap`, `evidence[]`, and optionally `void`,
  `voidReason`, `requirementIds[]`, `judgedAt`, `round` (which round this specific
  finding was judged in, if it differs from this generation's own `label` -- e.g. a
  finding carried forward unchanged), `unchanged` (true when this lane is that
  carried-forward case per S5 -- the wrapper UI uses this to collapse a repeated verdict
  instead of re-rendering the same evidence every round, so set it whenever it applies,
  don't leave it implicit)), and `failingRequirementIds[]`.

Start a new generation whenever you begin a new build/capture/judge round. Never rewrite
a past generation file -- if something about it turns out to be wrong, the correction
belongs in the next generation's record, not a retroactive edit.

## 8. Evidence discipline

Captures are content-addressed evidence: real files this round produced, referenced by
path in `lanes[].evidence[]`. A critic verdict without a citation to a capture file that
exists on disk from this round is void, and the round repeats -- no exceptions, no
"trust me." This applies to every lane, not just the visual ones: correctness cites
test-run output, performance cites the histogram/measurement file, gameplay/behavioral
cites the telemetry or replay capture that shows the described behavior.

The one exception is a lane carried forward per S5 (`unchanged: true`) -- its evidence
citation legitimately points at a file from whatever earlier round it was actually
captured in, not this one. That's the entire point of carrying it forward instead of
re-judging: the file already on disk from that earlier round is still valid evidence for
a build that hasn't changed since. Evidence-or-void still applies in full to any lane
that IS freshly judged this round.

## 9. Loop discipline

Build, capture, judge, triage and fix the round's top 3 gaps (S5), repeat. Don't stop after a fixed
number of rounds -- keep going until the human stops you. Periodically (roughly once per
major wave of work) run one fresh pass over the whole project specifically looking for
seams and inconsistencies between separately-improved pieces -- it fixes rough edges, it
does not redesign.

## 10. Session mechanics

The wrapper alerts the human whenever you're waiting on them -- you don't need to do
anything about that yourself. Keep `state.json`'s `statusNote` current; it's the human's
at-a-glance view of where things stand between sessions.
